===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|en|enm|hog}}, from {{inh|en|ang|hogg}}, {{m|ang|hocg|t=hog}}, possibly from {{der|en|non|hǫggva|t=to strike, chop, cut}}, from {{der|en|gem-pro|*hawwaną|t=to hew, forge}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*kewh₂-|t=to beat, hew, forge}}. 

Cognate with {{cog|goh|houwan}}, {{cog|osx|hauwan}}, {{cog|ang|hēawan}} (English {{m|en|hew}}). ''Hog'' originally meant a [[castrate]]d male pig, hence a sense of “the cut one”. (Compare ''[[hogget]]'' for a castrated male sheep.) More at {{l|en|hew}}.
Alternatively from a Brythonic language, from {{der|en|cel-pro|*sukkos}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*suH-}} and thus cognate with {{cog|cy|hwch||sow}} and {{cog|kw|hogh||pig}}.

