====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive|informal}} To [[greedily]] take more than one's share, to take precedence at the expense of another or others.
#: {{ux|en|Hey! Quit '''hogging''' all the blankets.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2000|author=Kate DiCamillo|title=Because of Winn-Dixie|publisher=Scholastic Inc.|location=New York|chapter=15
|passage=The [...] air-conditioning unit didn't work very good, and there was only one fan; and from the minute me and Winn-Dixie got in the library, he '''hogged''' it all.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[clip]] the [[mane]] of a [[horse]], making it short and bristly.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1880|author=William Day|title=The Racehorse in Training|passage= Some, perhaps, would wish to plait or shave the tail and crimp or '''hog''' the mane to complete the picture.}}
# {{lb|en|nautical}} To [[scrub]] with a hog, or scrubbing broom.
# {{lb|en|transitive|nautical}} To cause the [[keel]] of a ship to [[arch]] upwards (the opposite of {{m|en|sag}}).
#* {{quote-book
|year=1991
|author=J. E. Gordon
|title=Structures: Or Why Things Don't Fall Down
|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=8ltSZp6oQ8sC&pg=PA52
|isbn=9780141927695
|page=52
|publisher=Penguin UK
|1=en
|passage=Although most of the buoyancy of a ship is provided by the middle part of the hull and comparatively little by the tapering ends, nothing will ever prevent people from putting heavy weights into the ends of a ship. One result of this is that many vessels tend to ''''hog'''' (the two ends tend to droop and the middle of the hull tends to rise).}}
#* {{quote-book
|year=2013
|author=H. I. Lavery
|title=Shipboard Operations
|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=Be5SAQAAQBAJ&pg=PA267
|isbn=9781135386269
|page=267
|publisher=Routledge
|1=en
|passage=Difficulty may be encountered when securing cargo hatches on ships which '''hog''' or sag and the water-tight integrity of the ship may be impaired.}}
# {{lb|en|machining}} To take a rough cut, quickly removing material; to [[hog out]].

